    "created_at": "2018-08-29T18:11:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-08-29T18:11:21Z",
    "body": "Yeah, it should be 6/5/7, per <https://drafts.csswg.org/css-lists/#creating-counters>, because it seemed to be element-tree based.\r\n\r\nIt's not that it's useful, per se, it's that having special-case exceptions like this make it harder to reason about the platform.  Disallowing `list-item`, on *anything*, **doesn't actually reduce any complexity**.  All `display:list-item` does is the equivalent of setting up a `::before { content: counter(list-item); }` on the element; all of those individual pieces (`::before`, `counter()`) can still be done on a `legend` (and a `fieldset`, for that matter), and expose every aspect of the problem that you think `display:list-item` has.\r\n\r\nAlso, I can see a use-case for numbering legends across a form, for example.  A `legend` is just a `figcaption` or `caption` with magical display behavior; it fills the same semantic role, and those elements are often numbered, sometimes with CSS counters.",
